<Doc
  title="Privacy Policy"
  label="Mise · Legal"
  description="What Mise does with your sounds and your information: it keeps them on your devices and in your own Dropbox, and carries what you do between your devices through your own Apple Account."
  lede="Mise has no account with us and no analytics. Your sounds and everything about them stay on your devices and in storage you own. The one thing that reaches us is a report when the app crashes."
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <p>
    This policy covers the Mise app for iPhone, iPad and Mac, made by {COMPANY.name} (“we”, “us”), and these pages
    on azorean.io. It applies to nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>We never receive your sounds,</strong> their names, your tags, ratings, notes or recordings.</li>
    <li><strong>There is no Mise account.</strong> We hold no email, password or profile for you.</li>
    <li><strong>No analytics, no ads, no tracking,</strong> and nothing is sold or shared with advertisers.</li>
    <li><strong>When Mise crashes or freezes,</strong> it sends us a crash report through Sentry. It says where in Mise’s code it failed and on what kind of device, and never holds a sound or its name.</li>
    <li>Your library lives on your devices and, if you connect it, in <strong>your own Dropbox</strong>. What you do to a sound travels between your devices through <strong>your own Apple Account</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where your library lives</h2>
  <h3>On your devices</h3>
  <p>
    Mise keeps an index of your sounds on each device: names, folders, lengths, tempo, key, the families it has
    sorted them into, your marks, crates, likes and notes. Sounds are analyzed on the device itself; the audio is
    never sent anywhere to be analyzed.
  </p>
  <h3>In your Apple Account</h3>
  <p>
    So that your iPhone, iPad and Mac show the same library, Mise keeps what you change, and what it has worked out
    about each sound, in the private iCloud database of your own Apple Account: stars, crates, marks, notes, tempo,
    key, families. Your sound files are not kept there, and Mise does not use iCloud Drive. When your phone plays a
    sound that only your Mac has, the sound passes through that same private database. Only someone signed into
    your Apple Account can read any of it; we cannot. Apple’s privacy policy covers how iCloud stores it.
  </p>
  <h3>In your Dropbox</h3>
  <p>
    If you connect Dropbox, Mise signs in through Dropbox and reads and writes files in your account so your
    sounds can be listed, played and kept in step. The sign-in is stored in your device’s Keychain and, through
    iCloud Keychain, on your other devices. Mise talks to Dropbox directly; nothing passes through us. Dropbox’s
    privacy policy covers your files there. You can disconnect Mise at any time in Mise’s settings or in your
    Dropbox account’s connected apps.
  </p>

  <h2>What Mise asks your device for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Microphone</strong> — only to record, and only while you are on the Record page. Recordings are saved into your library.</li>
    <li><strong>Location</strong> — while you use the app, to note where a recording was made. That place is saved with the recording in your library and nowhere else.</li>
    <li><strong>Photos</strong> — only to delete a video from Photos after Mise has taken its audio, and only if you turn that on.</li>
    <li><strong>Local network</strong> — to find your own Mac running Mise, and the devices in a sesh, on the same Wi‑Fi.</li>
    <li><strong>System audio</strong> (Mac) — only to record what an app is playing, while you are on Record and have asked for it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Seshes</h2>
  <p>
    A sesh connects devices on the same Wi‑Fi. When you host or join one, the name you choose and the sounds you
    send are shared directly with the other devices in it, without passing through us. Leaving the sesh stops the
    sharing; sounds already sent to someone stay with them.
  </p>

  <h2>Crash reports and TestFlight</h2>
  <p>
    When Mise crashes, or stops responding for a couple of seconds, it sends a crash report to us through
    <a href="https://sentry.io">Sentry</a>, a service that collects crash reports for developers and keeps them on our
    behalf. A report holds where in Mise’s code the problem happened, the device model, the system version, Mise’s
    version, and what the app was doing in general terms, such as moving to the background. It never holds your
    sounds, their names, folders, tags or anything else in your library, and never your name, email or IP address.
    Crash reports are used only to fix Mise, are not linked to you, and are not used to track you. Sentry’s privacy
    policy covers how it stores them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Separately, if you have chosen in your device’s settings to share analytics with app developers, Apple may pass us crash
    reports for Mise. They describe the device and what the app was doing when it failed, and they contain no
    sounds. If you test Mise through TestFlight, Apple shares with us what TestFlight collects and any feedback you
    send. Apple’s privacy policy covers both.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Mise is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect information from them.</p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    Because everything is on your devices and in accounts you own, you are in control of all of it. Deleting Mise
    removes its data from that device. Mise’s data in iCloud can be deleted from your device’s settings, under your
    Apple Account’s iCloud storage. Files in your Dropbox stay yours to keep or delete. The only thing we hold is crash
    reports, which carry nothing that identifies you.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be posted here with a new effective date. If Mise ever starts
    collecting something it does not today, this page will say so before the app does.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about privacy go to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. {COMPANY.name},
    {COMPANY.addressLine}.
  </p>
